      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222738/assets/d4cf4f9e1cc145154e5f542a5064b51d6385e7f81d12c7187dff436f5e7e10f9.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs II and III do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graphs I and IV do have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graphs ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same pictures. This means that graphs I and IV show odd functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14222738/assets/60a38abf4723f137ef0f824613ab15282c9ff5e3098fac13173630baef577e45.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
